hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv \
-Dimporttsv.separator=, \
-Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,data:name,data:age,data:city,data:salary \
dataset_table \
/dataset/input/dataset_noheader.csv

----------------------------------
LOAD CSV FROM HDFS TO HBASE:
First create HBase table and column family: create 'dataset_table','data'  Example: create 'netflix','details'  Then ImportTsv: hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv -Dimporttsv.separator=, -Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,data:name,data:age,data:city,data:salary dataset_table /dataset/input/dataset_noheader.csv  Example: hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv -Dimporttsv.separator=, -Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,details:type,details:title,details:director,details:cast,details:country,details:date_added,details:release_year,details:rating,details:duration,details:listed_in,details:description netflix /ex6/input/netflix_cleaned.csv  HBASE_ROW_KEY = first CSV column used as row key; data:name means column family=data, column=name. The number and order of HBASE_ROW_KEY/column-family:column entries must match the dataset columns. Remove the CSV header before ImportTsv so the header does not become a row. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

START HBASE:
start-hbase.sh  → Start HBase services
jps  → Check for HMaster and HRegionServer
hbase shell  → Open HBase shell
exit  → Exit HBase shell
:contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

CREATE TABLE:
create 'table_name','column_family'
Example: create 'student','info'
Example: create 'dataset_table','data'
list  → List all HBase tables
describe 'table_name'  → Display table structure and column families
Example: describe 'student'
:contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3} :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

PUT / INSERT / UPDATE:
put 'table','rowkey','family:column','value'
Example: put 'student','101','info:name','Alice'
Example: put 'student','101','info:age','21'
Example: put 'lab_data','user1','details:name','Alice'
Example: put 'lab_data','user1','details:role','Security Analyst'
Same put command can update an existing cell by writing a new value.
:contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

GET:
get 'table','rowkey'
Example: get 'student','101'
Get one specific column:
get 'table','rowkey',{COLUMN => 'family:column'}
Example: get 'student','101',{COLUMN => 'info:name'}
:contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

SCAN:
scan 'table'  → Display all rows and columns
Example: scan 'student'
Display limited number of rows:
scan 'table',{LIMIT => 5}
Example: scan 'student',{LIMIT => 5}
:contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

COUNT:
count 'table'
Example: count 'student'
Used to count rows in a table.
:contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}

DELETE ONE CELL:
delete 'table','rowkey','family:column'
Example: delete 'student','101','info:age'
Example: delete 'lab_data','user1','details:role'
:contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}

DELETE ENTIRE ROW:
deleteall 'table','rowkey'
Example: deleteall 'student','101'

DROP TABLE:
disable 'table'
drop 'table'
Example: disable 'student'
Example: drop 'student'

TRUNCATE TABLE:
truncate 'table'
Example: truncate 'student'

FILTER — EQUAL TO:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',=,'binary:value')"}
Example: scan 'dataset_table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('data','city',=,'binary:Chennai')"}
Example: scan 'dataset_table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('data','age',=,'binary:21')"}
:contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}

FILTER — GREATER THAN:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',>,'binary:value')"}
Example: scan 'spotify',{FILTER=>"SingleColumnValueFilter('info','popularity',>,'binary:80')"}
Example: scan 'spotify',{FILTER=>"SingleColumnValueFilter('info','energy',>,'binary:0.9')"}
:contentReference[oaicite:11]{index=11}

FILTER — LESS THAN:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',<,'binary:value')"}
Example: scan 'spotify',{FILTER=>"SingleColumnValueFilter('info','popularity',<,'binary:50')"}

FILTER — GREATER THAN OR EQUAL:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',>=,'binary:value')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>=,'binary:2015')"}

FILTER — LESS THAN OR EQUAL:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',<=,'binary:value')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',<=,'binary:2020')"}

FILTER — SUBSTRING / CONTAINS:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column',=,'substring:value')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','listed_in',=,'substring:Action')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','country',=,'substring:United States')"}
:contentReference[oaicite:12]{index=12}

MULTIPLE CONDITIONS — AND:
scan 'table',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column1',=,'binary:value1') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('family','column2',>,'binary:value2')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','listed_in',=,'substring:Action') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>,'binary:2018')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','country',=,'substring:United States') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','duration',>,'binary:150')"}
Example: scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>=,'binary:2015') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',<=,'binary:2020')"}
Your uploaded material specifically notes that the shell-friendly form uses AND directly between the SingleColumnValueFilter conditions. :contentReference[oaicite:13]{index=13}

GET PARTICULAR ROW:
get 'dataset_table','1'
Example: get 'netflix','s1'
When the dataset's unique ID is the HBASE_ROW_KEY, get gives fast row access.
:contentReference[oaicite:14]{index=14}

GET PARTICULAR COLUMN:
get 'dataset_table','1',{COLUMN => 'data:name'}
Example: get 'student','101',{COLUMN => 'info:name'}
:contentReference[oaicite:15]{index=15}

DISPLAY ONLY SELECTED COLUMNS:
scan 'table',{COLUMNS => ['family:column1','family:column2']}
Example: scan 'netflix',{COLUMNS => ['details:title','details:director','details:country']}

START/STOP HBASE:
start-hbase.sh  → start HBase
stop-hbase.sh  → stop HBase

HBASE TABLE DESIGN:
Dataset example: id,name,category,value
Choose: ROW KEY = id
COLUMN FAMILY = info
Columns become: info:name, info:category, info:value
Create: create 'cat1_table','info'
:contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}

CSV TO HBASE MAPPING EXAMPLE:
CSV: id,name,age,city,salary
HBase mapping: HBASE_ROW_KEY,data:name,data:age,data:city,data:salary
Import command: hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv -Dimporttsv.separator=, -Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,data:name,data:age,data:city,data:salary dataset_table /dataset/input/dataset_noheader.csv
:contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}

IMPORT TSV WITH A COMMA SEPARATOR:
hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv -Dimporttsv.separator=, -Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,item:name,item:price bulk_table /input/data.csv
Example table first: create 'bulk_table','item'
:contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}

IMPORT TSV WITH PIPE SEPARATOR:
hbase org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.ImportTsv -Dimporttsv.separator='|' -Dimporttsv.columns=HBASE_ROW_KEY,details:type,details:title,details:director,details:cast,details:country,details:date_added,details:release_year,details:rating,details:duration,details:listed_in,details:description netflix /ex6/input/netflix_cleaned.csv
The lab cleans the CSV and converts it to pipe-separated format before ImportTsv.
:contentReference[oaicite:19]{index=19}

NETFLIX EXAMPLE:
create 'netflix','details'
exit
Then from Linux terminal run ImportTsv with show_id as HBASE_ROW_KEY and the remaining fields mapped to details:*.
Then return to HBase shell:
scan 'netflix'
:contentReference[oaicite:20]{index=20}

COMMON NETFLIX QUERIES:
Display all movies:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','type',=,'binary:Movie')"}
Get movie by Show ID:
get 'netflix','s1'
Display Title, Director, Country:
scan 'netflix',{COLUMNS => ['details:title','details:director','details:country']}
Display all TV Shows:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','type',=,'binary:TV Show')"}
Movies from India:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','country',=,'substring:India')"}
Movies released after 2020:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>,'binary:2020')"}
Movies duration > 120:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','duration',>,'binary:120')"}
Titles starting with The:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','title',=,'substring:The')"}
Action movies after 2018:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','listed_in',=,'substring:Action') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>,'binary:2018')"}
USA movies duration > 150:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','country',=,'substring:United States') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','duration',>,'binary:150')"}
Movies released 2015–2020:
scan 'netflix',{FILTER => "SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',>=,'binary:2015') AND SingleColumnValueFilter('details','release_year',<=,'binary:2020')"}
These Netflix operations are directly represented in your Experiment 6 material. :contentReference[oaicite:21]{index=21} :contentReference[oaicite:22]{index=22}

HBASE CRUD QUICK MEMORY:
CREATE → create 'table','family'
INSERT/UPDATE → put 'table','row','family:column','value'
READ ONE ROW → get 'table','row'
READ ALL → scan 'table'
COUNT → count 'table'
DELETE CELL → delete 'table','row','family:column'
DELETE ROW → deleteall 'table','row'
DESCRIBE → describe 'table'
LIST TABLES → list
DELETE TABLE → disable 'table' then drop 'table'
